sourceIssues.reason:
As printed, the sentence is false. F′ contains F, and H contains \overline{F}^{\ker\bar r}, which also contains F. Since F is a CM field, F ≠ ℚ, and two extensions of ℚ that share a subfield F ≠ ℚ cannot be linearly disjoint over ℚ.

The intended meaning is clear from the justification that follows the sentence. That justification computes L′LF^{suff}(ζ_N) ∩ F^{avoid} = ℚ, not an intersection involving F′. This is exactly what ACC+ Lemma 7.1.7 (Ann. of Math. 197, p. 1091) needs: the lemma takes a Galois extension E/ℚ that is linearly disjoint from H over ℚ, and draws its conclusion over FE. The sentence just before the misprint gives F′ = L′EF^{suff} = F·L′LF^{suff}(ζ_N).

Each equality in the displayed chain follows from facts established on pp. 1268–1272:
- F^{avoid} ⊂ F_0;
- L′ is linearly disjoint from F_0L;
- L is linearly disjoint from F_0;
- F^{avoid}F_2^{avoid}F^{suff} is linearly disjoint from ℚ(ζ_N);
- F^{suff} ∩ F^{avoid}F_2^{avoid} = ℚ.

All the fields involved are Galois over ℚ, so these intersections can be computed in the product Galois group. Because K is Galois, K ∩ F^{avoid} = ℚ gives linear disjointness from F^{avoid}, and hence from H ⊂ F^{avoid}. The argument is therefore correct once F′ is replaced by K.

Reading the sentence as "linearly disjoint over F" would make it true, but that is not the hypothesis of the cited lemma, so K is the right correction. The sentence before it, that F′ is linearly disjoint from \overline{F}^{\ker\bar r} over F, is correct and is not affected. This slip is separate from the question of whether l > n is needed to apply ACC+ Theorem 6.1.2 in the same proof.

sourceIssues.correction:
Also require l′ to be unramified in F^avoid. On p. 1268, F^avoid is defined as the normal closure over ℚ of F^av F̄^{ker r̄}(ζ_l), and l′ > 2ln + 5 > l. So the extra condition says exactly that l′ is unramified in F^av and in F̄^{ker r̄}. Since F ⊂ F^av, it also implies that l′ is unramified in F. It removes only finitely many primes, so the density argument in the first paragraph of the proof still works.

With this condition, the listed properties do supply everything that the first paragraph of the proof of [1, Corollary 7.2.4] needs when ℳ = {n − 1} and ℒ is the set of prime divisors of N:
- l′ ≥ 2(n + 1);
- E has good ordinary reduction at l′;
- r̄_{E,l′} has image GL_2(𝔽_{l′});
- l′ ∉ ℒ, because l′ ≡ 1 mod N;
- l′ is unramified in F^avoid.

The existence statement of Proposition 4.1 therefore stands.

sourceIssues.reason:
The proof of Proposition 4.1 applies the proof of [1, Corollary 7.2.4] with F^avoid as defined on p. 1268. The first paragraph of that proof (Annals version, p. 1100) chooses l “unramified in F^avoid”. It uses this at once with [1, Lemma 7.1.8(2)]: the field ℚ̄^{ker r̄_{E,l}} has no subextension other than ℚ that is unramified at l, so it is linearly disjoint from F^avoid over ℚ. The condition is used again on p. 1102, where F^avoid is shown to be linearly disjoint from L_3 “using that all of the primes in T ∪ {l} are unramified in F^avoid”. That is how F_2^avoid = F_1^avoid L_3 comes out disjoint from F^avoid.

Proposition 4.1 asks only that l′ be unramified in F. Nothing else in its list, in the conditions on N (p. 1268), or in the rest of Section 4 stops l′ from ramifying in F^av or in F̄^{ker r̄}. Surjectivity of r̄_{E,l′} on G_{F̃} only gives disjointness from F̃, not from F^av.

Counterexample. Fix F, n, l, r̄ and E, and choose N satisfying the conditions of p. 1268 for F^av = F. Then choose a prime p with:
- p ≡ 1 mod N and p > 2ln + 5;
- p unramified in F;
- r̄_{E,p}(G_{F̃}) = GL_2(𝔽_p);
- E has good ordinary reduction at p.
Such primes have positive density, by the same argument as the proof.

Now take F^av = F(ζ_p), which Theorem 1.1 allows. The primes ramified in F(ζ_p) are those ramified in F together with p. Since p ∤ N, the same N still satisfies every condition on p. 1268; the other conditions do not involve F^av. So l′ = p has all five listed properties. But ζ_p lies in F^av ⊂ F^avoid. It also lies in ℚ̄^{ker r̄_{E,p}}, because det r̄_{E,p} is a power of the mod-p cyclotomic character. Hence any F_2^avoid containing ℚ̄^{ker r̄_{E,p}} meets F^avoid in at least ℚ(ζ_p) ≠ ℚ. No field then satisfies both the first and the third conditions of the lower list, and the conclusion fails for this l′.

So the claim that the listed properties imply everything that paragraph needs is false. Proposition 4.1 asserts only that some l′ exists, and excluding finitely many more primes repairs the proof. The statement is unaffected.
